>> title: Sambousek
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: lamb, pastry, deep-fry, middle eastern, turnover, pine nuts
>> counters: Shawarma Counter
>> aka: sambousek, sambusak, sambousak, sambousick, sambousa, sambusa, sanbusak, meat turnovers, fried meat pies, half-moon pies
>> pairs-with: labneh, kabis, tahini-sauce
>> servings: 8
>> time: 1 hr 45 min
>> step.1: rub the butter into the flour
>> step.2: bring together, rest 30 min
>> step.3: fry the filling 8 min, cool it
>> step.4: fill, crimp the rim
>> step.5: fry at 350°F 4 min

Rub @unsalted butter{5%Tbs}(70 g; cold, diced) into @all-purpose flour{2 1/2%cups}(325 g), @fine sea salt{1%tsp}(6 g) and @baking powder{1/2%tsp} with your fingertips until it looks like coarse meal. Short and unleavened, not the yeasted dough the baked pastries take — this one has to fry to a crisp shell in four minutes, and a bread dough in hot oil goes to a doughnut.

Bring @&(~1)rubbed flour{} together with @cold water{1/2%cup}(120 mL) and @distilled white vinegar{1%tsp}(5 mL) into a stiff dough without kneading it, then ~rest{30%min} wrapped. The vinegar keeps it tender; kneading it develops gluten and gives you a tough half-moon that fights the bite.

Fry @ground lamb{10%oz}(280 g), @yellow onion{1%medium}(diced fine), @pine nuts{1/4%cup}(35 g), @ground allspice{1%tsp}, @ground cinnamon{1/2%tsp}, @kosher salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} in @olive oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) in a #skillet{} ~fry{8%min}, until the meat is browned and dry in the pan, then stir in @lemon juice{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and ~cool{20%min}. Dry in the pan is the target: a juicy filling steams the seam apart in the fryer.

Roll @&(~2)rested dough{} thin, cut four-inch rounds, spoon @&(~1)cooled filling{} onto one half of each, fold over and crimp the rim between finger and thumb in overlapping folds. Press out every pocket of air as you close it — trapped air expands in the oil and bursts the seam.

Fry @&(~1)filled sambousek{} in @neutral oil{5%cups}(1.2 L; at 350°F/175°C) in batches ~fry{4%min}, turning once, until they are evenly gold and blistered. They are just as good baked at 400°F for twenty minutes and they are not the same thing — baked, the shell is short and crumbly; fried, it shatters.
